3. Learned counsel for the petitioners would make the following submissions;
a) The lawful possession of the subject land having not been taken by the Competent Authority as specified under Section 10(5) or (6) of the Act, the petitioners are entitled for restoration of the subject land which is deemed to be vested with the Government under Section 10(3) of the ULC Act.
b) The restoration of the subject land cannot be deprived solely on the ground of delay in approaching the Court.

In support, he places reliance on the following decisions:
i. of the Apex Court in the case of Gajanan Kamlya Patil v. Addl. Collector &
Competent Authority, (2014) 12 SCC 523;
ii. of the Division Bench of this Court in the case of Mallaiah Basalingayya Hiremath v. State of Karnataka, 2011 SCC OnLine Kar 2553;
iii. Vaijayanti v. State of Karnataka, 2014 SCC OnLine Kar 10086; and NC:
2024:KHC:10590 iv. a decision of the coordinate Bench of this Court in the case of State v. Ningawa, 2021 SCC OnLine Kar 14864.
